<!DOCTYPE article PUBLIC "-//NLM//DTD Journal Archiving and Interchange DTD v2.3 20070202//EN" "archivearticle.dtd"><article xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" article-type="research-article"><?properties open_access?><front><journal-meta><journal-id journal-id-type="nlm-ta">J Exp Med</journal-id><journal-title>The Journal of Experimental Medicine</journal-title><issn pub-type="ppub">0022-1007</issn><issn pub-type="epub">1540-9538</issn><publisher><publisher-name>The Rockefeller University Press</publisher-name></publisher></journal-meta><article-meta><article-id pub-id-type="pmid">4959010</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="pmc">2138270</article-id><article-categories><subj-group subj-group-type="heading"><subject>Article</subject></subj-group></article-categories><title-group><article-title>PRESENCE OF TWO TYPES OF L POLYPEPTIDE CHAINS IN GUINEA PIG 7S IMMUNOGLOBULINS</article-title></title-group><contrib-group><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Nussenzweig</surname><given-names>Victor</given-names></name></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Lamm</surname><given-names>Michael E.</given-names></name></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Benacerraf</surname><given-names>Baruj</given-names></name></contrib></contrib-group><aff id="N0x1dda950N0x38a7730">From the Department of Pathology, New York University School of Medicine, New York</aff><pub-date pub-type="ppub"><day>31</day><month>10</month><year>1966</year></pub-date><volume>124</volume><issue>5</issue><fpage>787</fpage><lpage>803</lpage><history><date date-type="received"><day>3</day><month>6</month><year>1966</year></date></history><permissions><copyright-statement>Copyright &#x000a9; 1966 by The Rockefeller University Press</copyright-statement></permissions><abstract><p> Guinea pig 7S immunoglobulins (&#x003b3;<sub>1</sub> and &#x003b3;<sub>2</sub>) consist of two groups of molecules (K and L) bearing different types of L chains (&#x003ba; and &#x003bb;). Approximately one-third of the molecules in normal guinea pig &#x003b3;<sub>2</sub>-globulin bear the &#x003bb;- type of L chains, and all or most of the others bear &#x003ba;-chains. &#x003ba;- and &#x003bb;-chains have similar molecular weights. </p></abstract></article-meta></front></article>